<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">George
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Samuel
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Takashi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tohru
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Avihu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luis
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose self-speculative decoding for speech-aware LLMs by using the CTC encoder as a draft model to accelerate auto-regressive (AR) inference and improve ASR accuracy. Our three-step procedure works as follows: (1) if the frame entropies of the CTC output distributions are below a threshold, the greedy CTC hypothesis is accepted as final; (2) otherwise, the CTC hypothesis is verified in a single LLM forward pass using a relaxed acceptance criterion based on token likelihoods; (3) if verification fails, AR decoding resumes from the accepted CTC prefix. Experiments on nine corpora and five languages show that this approach can simultaneously accelerate decoding and reduce WER. On the HuggingFace Open ASR benchmark with a 1B parameter LLM and 440M parameter CTC encoder, we achieve a record 5.58% WER and improve the inverse real time factor by a factor of 4.4 with only a 12% relative WER increase over AR search. Code and model weights are publicly available under a permissive license.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>speech recognition, speech-aware LLM, speculative decoding
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>
IBM Research
</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_email"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">email: </span>gsaon@us.ibm.com</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a special case of attention encoder-decoder (AED) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, speech-aware language models (short SLMs) are currently the best performing ASR systems in terms of recognition accuracy. Indeed, the top five open models on the HuggingFace Open ASR leaderboard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> are SLMs with similar architectures. They all use conformer acoustic encoders trained with either RNN-T loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, CTC loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, or with next token cross-entropy loss as part of an AED ASR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and speech modality adapters which are either MLPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> or query transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The role of the adapters is to perform a temporal downsampling and to project the acoustic embeddings coming out of the encoder to a space that is interpretable by the LLM. For ASR, the LLM is trained to perform a repetition-like task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> in the sense that the embeddings of the output tokens can be seen as denoised versions of the speech embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One of the main drawbacks of AED models and, by extension, of SLMs is that inference is done auto-regressively, one token at a time, which requires one forward pass through the text LLM per generated token. This limits parallelism compared to non-autoregressive approaches where output tokens are predicted simultaneously such as CTC with greedy decoding, CTC with mask-predict error correction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> or iterative realignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Another class of models which have a good accuracy / inference speed tradeoff, termed semi-autoregressive, predict multiple output tokens at once within blocks of frames <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> or jointly predict tokens and durations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Accelerating AR inference for text LLMs via speculative decoding is an active area of research. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, the authors use a small draft AR model that runs in parallel to the large target model and produces <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> future tokens at a time conditioned on the current hypothesis which get verified by the target model in a single forward pass. If the verification fails, the target model falls back to AR decoding from the first position where the draft and target model distributions differ. The main idea in self speculative decoding is to reuse the target model itself as a draft model either by simultaneously predicting tokens at future positions <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1\ldots i+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1\ldots i+N</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> separate LM heads as in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> or by using predictions from intermediate layers (early exit) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An application of speculative decoding to ASR for transformer-based encoder-decoders is discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> where the authors train a small token and duration transducer (TDT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> decoder together with the frozen encoder which serves as a draft model for the larger causal transformer decoder. There are several differences between <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and our proposed work. First, we reuse the CTC encoder of the SLM instead of training a separate decoder for the draft model. Second, the authors focus on mitigating repetition errors whereas we show that complementary error patterns between CTC and SLM can actually improve WER. Third, we use a confidence metric given by the frame-level entropies of the CTC output distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> which allows skipping SLM verification entirely for confident CTC hypotheses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Other techniques that are relevant to our work are two-pass approaches that use a fast model for the first pass such as RNN-T or AR and either rescore candidate hypotheses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> or refine the output with a slower AR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and joint CTC/attention models with score fusion during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As to limitations of our proposed approach, we note that: (1) it requires an SLM with a CTC-trained encoder that is frozen during projector training and LoRA finetuning of the LLM; (2) our technique is limited to ASR and is not applicable to other speech tasks like translation or spoken question answering; (3) the SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis is utterance-based meaning that, if verification fails, the entire utterance has to be AR-decoded from the failed verification point<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          While it is possible to dynamically rejoin the CTC draft through local DP, we found this approach to be inefficient for batched inference.</span></span></span> which limits the gains for corpora with low acceptance rates. We mitigate (3) by using a relaxed acceptance criterion which requires the CTC hypothesis to be <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plausible</span> under the SLM distribution instead of an exact match of the SLM verification hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows: in section <a href="#S2" title="2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we describe our proposed method, in section <a href="#S3" title="3 Experiments and results ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we show some experimental evidence of its utility, and in section <a href="#S4" title="4 Conclusion and future work ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we summarize our findings and propose future directions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11243v1/cascade_figure.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/196;" width="476" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the proposed self-speculative decoding method for speech-aware LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method description</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we describe our method in detail specifically the CTC decoding and verification pass (subsection <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 CTC decoding and verification ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), the SLM verification pass (subsection <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), and the SLM auto-regressive fallback pass (subsection <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Auto-regressive fallback pass with CTC prefix ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>). The three steps as well as the chosen SLM architecture are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>CTC decoding and verification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to introduce some notations, we recall that the CTC formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> models the conditional distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{CTC}({\bf y}|{\bf x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{CTC}({\bf y}|{\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math> of an output sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{U})\in{\cal Y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝒴</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{U})\in{\cal Y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> given an input
sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{T})\in{\cal X}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝒳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{T})\in{\cal X}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq U</annotation></semantics></math>. The
elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous multidimensional
vectors whereas the elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to a discrete output space. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bf y}|{\bf x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bf y}|{\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed as a
sum over all possible alignments <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf a}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf a}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> that
are consistent with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{CTC}({\bf y}|{\bf x})=\sum_{{\bf a}\in{\cal B}^{-1}({\bf y})}p({\bf a}|{\bf x})=\sum_{{\bf a}\in{\cal B}^{-1}({\bf y})}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(a_{t}|h_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{CTC}({\bf y}|{\bf x})=\sum_{{\bf a}\in{\cal B}^{-1}({\bf y})}p({\bf a}|{\bf x})=\sum_{{\bf a}\in{\cal B}^{-1}({\bf y})}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(a_{t}|h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf h}=(h_{1},\ldots,h_{T})=Encoder({\bf x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}=(h_{1},\ldots,h_{T})=Encoder({\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of acoustic embeddings computed by an encoder and the elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf a}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the augmented vocabulary
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal Y}}={\cal Y}\cup\{\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒴</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal Y}}={\cal Y}\cup\{\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (called blank) denotes the null output. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal B}</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping that removes consecutive duplicates and blanks (in this order) such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal B}({\bf a})={\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal B}({\bf a})={\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute the draft CTC hypothesis as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}={\cal B}(\hat{{\bf a}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}={\cal B}(\hat{{\bf a}})</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf a}}=(\hat{a}_{1},\ldots,\hat{a}_{T})=(\mathop{\rm argmax}_{a\in\overline{{\cal Y}}}p(a|h_{1}),\ldots,\mathop{\rm argmax}_{a\in\overline{{\cal Y}}}p(a|h_{T}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf a}}=(\hat{a}_{1},\ldots,\hat{a}_{T})=(\mathop{\rm argmax}_{a\in\overline{{\cal Y}}}p(a|h_{1}),\ldots,\mathop{\rm argmax}_{a\in\overline{{\cal Y}}}p(a|h_{T}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">denoting the greedy alignment path. The draft hypothesis is accepted as final if all the frame-level entropies of the CTC output distribution are less than a threshold i.e.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{a\in\overline{\cal Y}}-p(a|h_{t})\log p(a|h_{t})&lt;\tau_{CTC}\qquad\forall t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a\in\overline{\cal Y}}-p(a|h_{t})\log p(a|h_{t})&lt;\tau_{CTC}\qquad\forall t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In comparison to (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 CTC decoding and verification ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), SLMs model the conditional distribution of the output sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> given the input sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}</annotation></semantics></math> differently:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{SLM}({\bf y}|{\bf x})=\prod_{i=1}^{U}p(y_{i}|{\bf y}_{&lt;i},{\bf x})=\prod_{i=1}^{U}p(y_{i}|{\bf y}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>U</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>U</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{SLM}({\bf y}|{\bf x})=\prod_{i=1}^{U}p(y_{i}|{\bf y}_{&lt;i},{\bf x})=\prod_{i=1}^{U}p(y_{i}|{\bf y}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf g}=(g_{1},\ldots,g_{T^{\prime}})=Adapter({\bf h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf g}=(g_{1},\ldots,g_{T^{\prime}})=Adapter({\bf h})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, is a sequence of LLM-compatible acoustic embeddings computed by a speech modality adapter that takes as input the embedding sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}</annotation></semantics></math> computed by the CTC encoder. The draft CTC hypothesis <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> is accepted during the SLM verification pass if all the token likelihoods under the SLM output distribution are greater than a threshold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\hat{y}_{i}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})&gt;\tau_{SLM}\qquad\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,|\hat{\bf y}|\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\hat{y}_{i}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})&gt;\tau_{SLM}\qquad\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,|\hat{\bf y}|\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, because of the causal attention mask of the text LLM, (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be computed in parallel for all tokens with a single forward pass through the LLM. The reason why we use entropy in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 CTC decoding and verification ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and likelihood in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is because the specific hypothesis <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be verified for the latter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Auto-regressive fallback pass with CTC prefix</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) does not hold, we find the longest verified CTC prefix <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j=\min\{i\in\{1,\ldots,|\hat{\bf y}|\}~s.t.~p(\hat{y}_{i}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})\leq\tau_{SLM}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.497em">.</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=\min\{i\in\{1,\ldots,|\hat{\bf y}|\}~s.t.~p(\hat{y}_{i}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;i},{\bf g})\leq\tau_{SLM}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and simply continue auto-regressive generation from there:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\bf y}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{{\bf y}\in{\cal Y}^{*}}p_{SLM}({\bf y}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j},{\bf x})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝒴</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>𝐒𝐋𝐌</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>𝐣</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\bf y}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{{\bf y}\in{\cal Y}^{*}}p_{SLM}({\bf y}|\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j},{\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the final hypothesis that is output by the self-speculative decoding (short SSD) process is</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}_{f}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\hat{\bf y},&amp;\mathrm{if}~(\ref{eq:CTC-verif})~\mathrm{or}~(\ref{eq:SLM-verif})\\
(\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j},\overline{\bf y}),&amp;\mathrm{otherwise}\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.330em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 CTC decoding and verification ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>or</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 SLM verification of the CTC hypothesis ‣ 2 Method description ‣ Self-Speculative Decoding for LLM-based ASR with CTC Encoder Drafts" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>otherwise</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}_{f}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\hat{\bf y},&amp;\mathrm{if}~(\ref{eq:CTC-verif})~\mathrm{or}~(\ref{eq:SLM-verif})\\
(\hat{{\bf y}}_{&lt;j},\overline{\bf y}),&amp;\mathrm{otherwise}\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparing full AR and self-speculative decoding for granite-speech-4.0-1b. High accuracy: <math id="S2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{CTC}}\!=\!0.7,\tau_{\text{SLM}}\!=\!0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>CTC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>SLM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{CTC}}\!=\!0.7,\tau_{\text{SLM}}\!=\!0.2</annotation></semantics></math>. High RTFx: <math id="S2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{CTC}}\!=\!3.0,\tau_{\text{SLM}}\!=\!0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>CTC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>SLM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{CTC}}\!=\!3.0,\tau_{\text{SLM}}\!=\!0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. %C/%L = CTC/LLM acceptance rates at each verification stage. RTFx are measured on 1 H100. <math id="S2.T1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}p&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}p&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{\dagger}p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\dagger}p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> (Statistical significance was assessed using two-proportion z-test).</figcaption>
